CONVERSATION:
is there anybody here that can help with a pyqt problem?
er, it might be a qt problem.  :)
See, I install pyqt3 with qt3.1 (I think it is), and my pyqt app works fine
Then I install pyqt4 with qt4.1.somethingorother, and my pyqt3 app segfaults
start an interactive python interpreter, and do import qt (which imports pyqt3), and I get a segfault
has anyone encountered this before?
Lucifer_arma: with the PyQt 4 beta at one point... don't remember exactly what the problem was
Lucifer_arma: but I'm pretty sure the import for PyQt 4 isn't &quot;qt&quot; anymore
it's not, import qt should import pyqt3, which is what I want, the app is still a pyqt3 app
It's import PyQt4 or something for 4
yeah, like from PyQt4 import QtGui
right.  I'm trying to continue development on a pyqt3 app and start development on a pyqt4 app.  Ultimately I'll port the pyqt3 app to pyqt4, but I need to continue working on it like it is and can't take the time to restart the gui
all my qt 3 apps work fine, still.  :)  KDE user here, I'd notice if my qt3 apps stopped working
yeah Qt 4 doesn't interfere with Qt 3. I don't know what's up with PyQt there.
I originally thought it was just me, and I don't use PyQt enough to care about it not working for 3 :)
maybe there's a mailing list to ask on, where the riverbank folks can comment on it.
there's a mailing list, I guess I'll take it there.  I find irc channels generally more helpful than mailing lists, though.  ;)
at least it's not just me, that means maybe someone else has encountered *and* fixed it :)
I think a lot of people in this channel use C++ for Qt a lot. At least I can only think of one person who regularly uses PyQt in this channel.
at least who admits it ;-)
heh
I might switch to C++ instead, if I could get it to build easily from a gnu autotools-run project
qt's biggest asset, imo, is the designer.  Once you start with that, the underlying language isn't so important anymore
Lucifer_arma: why bother with autotools in Qt 4?
at least CMake is coming along if you don't like QMake
because I like them a lot
I know, it's crazy, but I actually like gnu's autotools
well, it *is* also popularly know as autohell
yep
and I've had projects I worked on build in bsd, linux, cygwin, and mac os x by virtue of the &quot;autohell&quot;
there's some inertia involved here, too, don't worry :)
but usually more hell for the poor sap trying to run ./configure I find :-D
./configure --path-to-libs=/the/path... ok version and everything, but still &quot;Cannot frind craplib v 2.0.4&quot;
my own experience is that that's usually a poorly written configure check
apparently some people like to cut and paste config scripts, and understand the finer points of making them later.
*them work later
well, tht's not the tools fault.  Just because I use my biggest wrench as a hammer occasionally doesn't mean it's a sucky wrench :)
it's the fault in so much as people feel the need to resort to doing that :)
I haven't looked at CMake, but the other alternatives I've seen have used mostly wonkety xml files
I mean the info docs for autotools are a mess, the &quot;goat book&quot; might as well be the &quot;goatse book&quot;.. IMHO of course
found one --&gt; hello_world: prints hello world
masterfully done
how do I build it?
will it support uclibc?
./configure --standard-greeting=1 --support-preansi=0 --ncLp=x23 --lang=en; make; make install
actually, there are some very excellent open source documentation around.  ecasound's docs are freaking wonderful and detailed, just hard to read
ah, I see you got GNU Hello World
and Qt's own documentation is nothing to sneer at
nope, main reason I chose to start with Qt over Gtk when first learning them
heh.  Didn't even look at any gtk code?
honestly, I didn't get that far.  I've only seen gtk code by accident, and it's quite traumatic, but I picked Qt because it's easier to learn and it's a lot more stable
I'm not sure I understand what you mean by "easy to learn"
I mean, I'm not sure I understand what you mean by "easy to learn"
